  <section class="prose page-guide" style="margin-top:44px">
    <h2>How to choose where to study</h2>
    <p>The best country for you depends on your field, your budget and the kind of funding you can win. Some countries are known for large government scholarships, such as Chevening in the United Kingdom, DAAD in Germany, MEXT in Japan, the Global Korea Scholarship in South Korea, Stipendium Hungaricum in Hungary and the Chinese Government Scholarship. Others, like Germany and Norway, have low or no tuition at public universities, so a smaller scholarship can go further.</p>
    <p>Think about the language of teaching, the cost of living, the length of the degree and whether you can work while you study. A one-year master's in the United Kingdom costs less in total than a two-year master's elsewhere, while countries like the Netherlands, Sweden and Germany often treat PhD students as paid employees.</p>
    <p>Whichever country you choose, read the eligibility rules for your nationality, apply on the official website only, and never pay anyone who promises a guaranteed scholarship.</p>
  </section>
